index 02d332182..7b116585d 100644
--- a/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
+++ b/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
@@ -64,6 +64,7 @@ safe measurement posture.
 | `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST` | **ON by device probe when unset on AMD, OFF on every other vendor** — the only vendor this was measured on. Specialization constant 3 of `mul_mat_vecq.comp`; `1` = decode each row's weight block once per (row, k-slice) into registers and loop the columns doing only the B load and the integer dot, `0` = the inherited nest, which re-runs the whole A decode once per column. Only an exact `0` or `1` is taken; anything else falls back to the probe. Added 2026-09-14 (lane-235/R48b). | Scope: every `q8_1` integer-dot `mul_mat_vec` pipeline. Both nests call the same `mmvq_load_a()` / `mmvq_dot_a()` pair in the same order, so results are **bit-identical by construction** and this is a scheduling change only. `NUM_COLS == 1` folds to the inherited nest at pipeline-compile time. Probed once at device creation, because routing reads the same answer. Two narrowings ride on top: `IQ1_S`/`IQ1_M` are OFF by default on register grounds (see the next row) and the legacy-block family (`Q4_0`, `Q4_1`, `Q5_0`, `Q5_1`, `Q8_0`, `MXFP4`) is OFF at `NUM_COLS >= 6`. **`=0` is the widest switch in this group: it removes the hoisted nest AND the R48b routing that depends on it.** | Default. Use `=0` for the OFF arm of a pairing — one binary serves both arms — or on a vendor this has not been measured on. | **Correctness CLEARED, default-route performance UNMEASURED.** `test-backend-ops MUL_MAT -b Vulkan0` is 0 FAIL in both arms, and two planted reds proved the decode site live (RED-1, fires in both arms) and the hoisted nest the one serving (RED-2, red ON / green OFF). HQ forced-MMVQ pairing (`32-paired-r48bforce.txt`, 3 quiet interleaved rounds, same binary): the hoist removes a **10-15x tail cliff** at `NUM_COLS` 6..7 for `q4_K` (248320x5120 `5.1 / 3.3` → `52 / 48` GB/s; 5120x17408 `3.7 / 2.5` → `26.8 / 24.3`) and holds S-X8 flat at the bus across `n=1..8`. It **REGRESSES Q8_0 at `n>=6`** (`-25 / -17 / -24%` at `n=6/7/8` on 4096x14336, `-17%` at `n=8` on 5120x17408, `-10%` at `n=6` on 248320x5120), which is what the legacy-family `NUM_COLS >= 6` gate is for. `q5_K` at `n=1`, its only MMVQ width, is a wash. Every number here is from a `GGML_VK_FORCE_MMVQ=1` arm; a **default-routing re-pair is owed**. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST_IQ1` | **OFF when unset.** Only an exact `1` turns the A-side hoist on for the `IQ1_S` and `IQ1_M` `q8_1` pipelines, and never above the global switch — `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST=0` forces it off regardless. Added 2026-09-14 (lane-235/R48b). | Scope: the `IQ1_S` and `IQ1_M` `q8_1` `mul_mat_vec` pipelines only. They carry the largest A state of any MMVQ type (10 and 12 registers per row against 5-6 for the K-quants) and run the most rows per workgroup (`rm_iq_int` = 4 or 8), the highest product in the register table. Gated in the HOST, not by an `#ifdef`: the shader keeps both paths, so no rebuild moves it. | Only to pair the IQ1 types specifically. | **UNMEASURED on these two types — that is exactly why they ship OFF.** The default is a register-pressure judgement (report section 3 of `OPUS-R48B-MMVQ-A-HOIST-REPORT.md`), not a measurement. Arithmetic is identical either way; correctness is covered by the same `MUL_MAT` sweep. |
 | `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_ROUTE` | **unset = R48b routing.** Only the exact string `legacy` pins `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` back to the pre-R48b rules while KEEPING the hoisted nest. Added 2026-09-14 (lane-235/R48b phase 2). | Scope: two AMD routing rules, and nothing else. (i) `GGML_TYPE_SX8` → MMVQ at every width (`legacy`: opt-in via `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_MMVQ=1`). (ii) `GGML_TYPE_Q4_K` at `n >= 5` → MMVQ when `k <= 8192`, f32 above (`legacy`: f32 at every `n >= 5`). `Q5_K`, `Q8_0` and the legacy-block family are untouched in both modes. Both rules are additionally gated on the hoist being live, so on a non-AMD device or under `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST=0` they are already the legacy rules. **`legacy` is NOT the same arm as `GGML_ARIFI_MMVQ_A_HOIST=0`**: this one keeps the hoisted nest and changes only where traffic is sent, the other removes the nest as well. A pairing log must say which was used. | Use `legacy` to pair the ROUTING change alone, holding the kernel constant. | **Routing derived from the HQ forced-MMVQ pairing, not yet re-measured at default routing.** `q4_K` MMVQ+hoist vs the f32 default at `n=5..8`: 248320x5120 (k=5120) `55.7/52.1/48.0/45.6` vs `46.0/36.1/35.0/29.0` GB/s (**+21/+44/+37/+57%**), 17408x5120 (k=5120) `35.7/34.8/33.5/31.3` vs `33.2/27.9/29.9/24.4` (win), 5120x17408 (**k=17408**) `32.4/26.8/24.3/22.7` vs `37.0/30.8/31.6/27.2` (**LOSS**) — hence the `k` split. `k = 8192` is the midpoint of the two measured `k` values and is an **untested boundary**: nothing was run between 5120 and 17408. S-X8 evidence is in the `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_MMVQ` row above. |
+| `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA` / `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA_FILE` | **ON when a PCA companion GGUF is present, and there is nothing to turn on when there is not.** `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA=0` makes the loader skip the companion entirely; `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA_FILE=<path>` names one instead of the auto-discovered `<model>.sx8pca.gguf` beside the model. Added 2026-09-15 (lane-242/R53). | Scope: `GGML_TYPE_SX8` weights reached through `build_lora_mm`. S-X8 v4.3 stores two signed-4-bit PCA coefficients in byte 29 of every 30-byte block, but the PCA bases and scales live only in the `.sx8v43` container - the author's GGUF converter drops them and his llama.cpp patch ignores the coefficients. `tools/sx8/extract_sx8_pca.py` pulls the bases/scales out of the container over HTTP ranges and `tools/sx8/add_sx8_pca_to_gguf.py` writes them, with the unpacked coefficient planes, into a companion GGUF (366 MB for the 4B, against a 3.9 GB rewrite of the model file). The correction is built from stock ggml ops in `src/llama-graph.cpp` (`Z0[kb] = s0*sum_t X*b0`, `Y[n] += c0*Z0 + c1*Z1`), so CPU, Vulkan and any future backend get it from one place and no kernel or shader knows about PCA. **With no companion file the map is empty and the graph is the pre-R53 graph, bit for bit.** One receipt line at load names how many tensors were attached. | Leave it alone when a companion is beside the model. Use `=0` for the OFF arm of a quality pairing on the same binary and the same files. | **QUALITY MEASURED, speed UNMEASURED.** `test-backend-ops MUL_MAT` is 0 FAIL in both arms (its synthetic tensors carry no companion, so the arms are the same code). `test-sx8-pca` runs a real 64-row slice of `blk.0.ffn_down.weight` against float64 goldens taken straight from the spec: the PCA term lands within `7.3e-07` of the output on CPU and `4.3e-08` on Vulkan, and the two backends agree to `8.2e-07`. A planted coefficient-sign flip moves the term by `1.5x` and is caught. The pre-existing `2.159e-03` core gap against an exact decode is the Q8_1 activation quantization of the S-X8 mat-mul, identical on both backends and untouched by R53. PPL wikitext-2 on the 4B (`llama-perplexity -c 512`, 580 chunks, one cell at a time): S-X8 PCA OFF **9.9989**, PCA ON **9.9955** (**-0.034%**), Q8_0 **9.9742**. The correction is oriented correctly -- it moves the weights TOWARD a Q8_0 reference on every tensor tested (-3.0% to -3.4% RMS error), while a sign flip and a c0/c1 swap both move them away -- but it does **NOT** reproduce the -2.64% of the author's Table 4. On this file the dropped PCA coefficients are worth ~0.03% of perplexity. Absolute values are not comparable to his: he scores 512-token windows at stride 128, `llama-perplexity` does not. **Not applied to the embedding LOOKUP** (`token_embd` via `get_rows`), which is not a matmul - that arm of the correction is OWED. |
 | `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_ROWS` / `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_WG` | **unset = the probed default** (workgroup = the device subgroup width when it is a multiple of 32, else 32; rows = 2). Read once at pipeline creation. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-232/R47a), extended 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit D). | Scope: every S-X8 `mul_mat_vec` pipeline, f32/f16/q8_1/id alike. An override is taken WHOLE or refused WHOLE against this device's real limits: workgroup a positive multiple of 32, within `maxComputeWorkGroupInvocations`, and `NUM_COLS * rows * wg * 4` within `maxComputeSharedMemorySize` at the WIDEST `NUM_COLS`. **Setting either variable also switches the R46b shape-tuned lookup OFF for the whole run** — a run that kept the measured table on the cells the arm does not name would be a mixed arm. A stderr receipt names APPLIED or REFUSED once per distinct pair. | Sweep instrument. Use it to pair rows 1/2/4/8 against workgroups 64/128/256 out of one binary, or to pin the pre-R46b behaviour for an A/B. Not a serve setting. | On the 780M (subgroup 64, 32 KiB shared) `rows=8/wg=256` is REFUSED by shared memory and the run keeps the default; `rows=4/wg=256` and `rows=8/wg=128` sit exactly on the 32,768-byte limit, are admitted, and measured 12/12 GREEN on `MUL_MAT` (`research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-11-lane-232-r47a-alloc-instrument/33-boundary-rows4-wg256.txt`, `34-boundary-rows8-wg128.txt`). |
 | S-X8 mat-vec shape tuning (R47d lookup) | **Code, not an environment variable. ON by device probe** — subgroup width 64 with `maxComputeSharedMemorySize == 32768`, the SUBGROUP workgroup class, f32 activations, and only for the exact (m, k, n) cells the table names. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit D). | Scope: `GGML_TYPE_SX8` `mul_mat_vec` with f32 activations. 31 measured cells over the four real 27B S-X8 decode shapes at n=1..8; the n=1 FFN orientations take rows=4/wg=256 and the 248320x5120 output takes rows=8/wg=64, which is why one global pair cannot express it. Every other shape, every n>8, the f16/q8_1/id paths and every other device keep the probed default. A stderr receipt at pipeline creation names ON/OFF and the probed default. | Nothing to operate. To measure against it, set `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_ROWS`/`_WG` (which turns the lookup off whole). | **MEASURED on lane-232's base, which does NOT carry R46's `n <= 3` whole-block decode.** Every selected cell read >= 0.97 of the probed default in BOTH rounds, best cells +12.18% (248320x5120 n=2) and +10.76% (n=6); source `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-11-lane-232-r47a-alloc-instrument/R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md`, "Sweep selection". R46 replaces the decode math and row-blocking loop at `n <= 3` and leaves the reduction shape untouched, so the shared-memory arithmetic still holds, but **the n=1/2/3 optima are UNVERIFIED on the rebased kernel** until they are re-measured on the R46b stage. No held-out deployment guarantee is claimed. |
 | `GGML_VK_PLACEMENT=bulk-large-heap` | **OFF when unset; unset changes no byte of the existing placement chain.** Only the exact string `bulk-large-heap` is accepted. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit E). | Scope: the bulk device buffers the model loader suballocates weights from (`ggml_backend_vk_buffer_type_alloc_buffer`), and nothing else — prealloc scratch and staging keep their current paths. The policy asks for a `DEVICE_LOCAL` type on the LARGEST heap and excludes every other heap for those buffers; grouping is untouched. A refusal is not fatal: that one buffer falls back to the stock chain and says so on stderr. A receipt names the chosen heap **with tracing off**. | Only to test the R47d placement hypothesis, in proof cells A/B, against an unchanged-policy control. | **UNMEASURED as a fix — commissioned, not proven.** R47d established the mechanism it targets: the placement chain tests each allocation against the WHOLE heap size with no cumulative accounting (`ggml-vulkan.cpp` `ggml_vk_find_memory_properties`), so `GGML_VK_PREFER_HOST_MEMORY=1` puts bulk weights on host-visible heap 1 and a 256 MiB grouping puts them in the small combined-property heap 2, and the first pinned upload then dies at `submit seq=0` with `ErrorUnknown` in both arms (`R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md`, "Exact failure boundaries"). The driver-internal cause of `ErrorUnknown` is **UNSCORED**. The cumulative requirement reservation with rollback that the same commission asks for is **NOT implemented**; this is heap selection only. |
diff --git a/src/llama-graph.cpp b/src/llama-graph.cpp
index adf766dae..8d2d39d24 100644
--- a/src/llama-graph.cpp
+++ b/src/llama-graph.cpp
@@ -1651,6 +1651,44 @@ ggml_tensor * llm_graph_context::build_lora_mm(
 
     ggml_tensor * res = ggml_mul_mat(ctx0, w, cur);
 
+    // ArifiLabs lane-242 / R53: the S-X8 v4.3 PCA correction.
+    //
+    // Every 30-byte S-X8 block carries two signed-4-bit PCA coefficients (c0, c1) that the
+    // llama.cpp decoders throw away, costing his Table 4 gap: PPL 10.5043 without them against
+    // 10.2267 with. The correction rides on the linearity of the dot product
+    // (SX8_FLASH_V4_3_SPEC.md section 5, sx8_decode1_v3.cu:8-13):
+    //
+    //     Z0[kb] = s0[kb] * sum_t X[kb*32+t] * b0[kb][t]         (once per token, per matmul)
+    //     Y[n]  += sum_kb c0(kb,n)*Z0[kb] + c1(kb,n)*Z1[kb]
+    //
+    // so the per-weight PCA cost collapses to two FMAs per 32-weight block. Expressed here in
+    // stock ggml ops, which is what makes it backend-agnostic: no CPU kernel and no shader has
+    // to learn about PCA, and CPU, Vulkan and any future backend all get it from one place.
+    // s0/s1 are pre-folded into b0/b1 by tools/sx8/add_sx8_pca_to_gguf.py, so the scale multiply
+    // is already paid at build time.
+    //
+    // Without a PCA companion GGUF the map is empty and this is not reached at all.
+    if (w->type == GGML_TYPE_SX8 && mdl != nullptr) {
+        if (const auto * pca = mdl->get_sx8_pca(w)) {
+            const int64_t n_cb = pca->b0->ne[1];
+            const int64_t n_tk = ggml_nelements(cur) / cur->ne[0];
+
+            // [K, n_tk] -> [32, n_cb, n_tk]; b0 is [32, n_cb, 1] and broadcasts over the tokens.
+            ggml_tensor * xc = ggml_is_contiguous(cur) ? cur : ggml_cont(ctx0, cur);
+            ggml_tensor * xr = ggml_reshape_3d(ctx0, xc, 32, n_cb, n_tk);
+
+            ggml_tensor * z0 = ggml_sum_rows(ctx0, ggml_mul(ctx0, xr, pca->b0)); // [1, n_cb, n_tk]
+            ggml_tensor * z1 = ggml_sum_rows(ctx0, ggml_mul(ctx0, xr, pca->b1));
+
+            z0 = ggml_reshape_2d(ctx0, z0, n_cb, n_tk);
+            z1 = ggml_reshape_2d(ctx0, z1, n_cb, n_tk);
+
+            // c is [n_cb, N], so each mat-vec contributes one of the two rank-1 terms.
+            res = ggml_add(ctx0, res, ggml_mul_mat(ctx0, pca->c0, z0));
+            res = ggml_add(ctx0, res, ggml_mul_mat(ctx0, pca->c1, z1));
+        }
+    }
+
     if (w_s) {
         res = ggml_mul(ctx0, res, w_s);
     }
diff --git a/src/llama-model-loader.cpp b/src/llama-model-loader.cpp
index f351d4963..bf74dc7bd 100644
--- a/src/llama-model-loader.cpp
+++ b/src/llama-model-loader.cpp
@@ -846,6 +846,56 @@ llama_model_loader::llama_model_loader(
 
             LLAMA_LOG_INFO("%s: additional %d GGUFs metadata loaded.\n",  __func__, n_split - 1);
         }
+
+        // ArifiLabs lane-242 / R53: optional S-X8 v4.3 PCA companion GGUF.
+        //
+        // The .sx8v43 container carries per-tensor PCA bases and scales that the author's GGUF
+        // converter drops, so a GGUF-only engine cannot apply the correction and pays his
+        // Table 4 gap (PPL 10.5043 instead of 10.2267). They are ~0.4% of the weights, so they
+        // ride in a companion file rather than forcing a rewrite of a 26 GB GGUF. Discovered as
+        // "<model>.sx8pca.gguf" beside the model, or named by GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA_FILE.
+        // GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA=0 skips the companion entirely -- the no-companion path is the
+        // pre-R53 path, bit for bit.
+        //
+        // Its tensors join weights_map exactly like a split's, so loading, mmap, backend
+        // placement and upload all take the ordinary route.
+        const char * pca_env = getenv("GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA");
+        if (!(pca_env && pca_env[0] == '0' && pca_env[1] == '\0')) {
+            std::string pca_fname;
+            if (const char * e = getenv("GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA_FILE")) {
+                pca_fname = e;
+            } else {
+                std::string base = fname;
+                if (base.size() > 5 && base.compare(base.size() - 5, 5, ".gguf") == 0) {
+                    base.resize(base.size() - 5);
+                }
+                const std::string cand = base + ".sx8pca.gguf";
+                if (FILE * probe = ggml_fopen(cand.c_str(), "rb")) {
+                    fclose(probe);
+                    pca_fname = cand;
+                }
+            }
+
+            if (!pca_fname.empty()) {
+                struct ggml_context * pca_ctx = NULL;
+                struct gguf_init_params pca_params = {
+                    /*.no_alloc = */ true,
+                    /*.ctx      = */ &pca_ctx,
+                };
+                gguf_context_ptr pca_gguf { gguf_init_from_file(pca_fname.c_str(), pca_params) };
+                if (!pca_gguf) {
+                    throw std::runtime_error(format("%s: failed to load S-X8 PCA companion from %s",
+                                __func__, pca_fname.c_str()));
+                }
+
+                split_metadata.emplace_back(std::move(pca_gguf));
+                gguf_contexts.push_back(split_metadata.back().get());
+                files.emplace_back(new llama_file(pca_fname.c_str(), "rb", use_direct_io));
+                contexts.emplace_back(pca_ctx);
+
+                LLAMA_LOG_INFO("%s: S-X8 PCA companion: %s\n", __func__, pca_fname.c_str());
+            }
+        }
     } else if (file != nullptr) {
         struct ggml_context * ctx = NULL;
         struct gguf_init_params params = {
@@ -1609,6 +1659,46 @@ struct ggml_tensor * llama_model_loader::create_tensor(
         n_created++;
     }
 
+    // ArifiLabs lane-242 / R53: an S-X8 weight pulls in its PCA correction companions, when a
+    // companion GGUF supplied them. Doing it HERE rather than per-architecture (the escha_aux
+    // pattern in llama-model.cpp) means every architecture that can hold an S-X8 weight is
+    // covered by one block, and the companions inherit this weight's buffer type for free.
+    //
+    // A TENSOR_DUPLICATED weight (token_embd reused as the tied output) re-enters under the same
+    // NAME, but lands in a second buffer-type context as a different ggml_tensor. So the guard is
+    // by name: the second arrival shares the companions the first created instead of loading them
+    // twice, which would overshoot done_getting_tensors' count.
+    if (tensor->type == GGML_TYPE_SX8 && sx8_pca.find(tensor) == sx8_pca.end()) {
+        const auto seen = sx8_pca_by_name.find(tn.str());
+        if (seen != sx8_pca_by_name.end()) {
+            sx8_pca.emplace(tensor, seen->second);
+            return tensor;
+        }
+
+        const int64_t n_cb = tensor->ne[0] / 32;   // blocks-of-K per row
+        const int64_t n_r  = tensor->ne[1];        // output rows
+
+        // c0 decides: absent means this build has no PCA companion and the correction is off.
+        ggml_tensor * c0 = create_tensor(hparams, buft_list_cpu, buft_list_input, buft_list_output,
+                buft_list_layer, LLM_TN_IMPL(tn.arch, tn.tensor, "sx8_pca_c0", tn.bid, tn.xid),
+                { n_cb, n_r }, TENSOR_NOT_REQUIRED);
+        if (c0 != nullptr) {
+            sx8_pca_weights w;
+            w.c0 = c0;
+            w.c1 = create_tensor(hparams, buft_list_cpu, buft_list_input, buft_list_output,
+                    buft_list_layer, LLM_TN_IMPL(tn.arch, tn.tensor, "sx8_pca_c1", tn.bid, tn.xid),
+                    { n_cb, n_r }, 0);
+            w.b0 = create_tensor(hparams, buft_list_cpu, buft_list_input, buft_list_output,
+                    buft_list_layer, LLM_TN_IMPL(tn.arch, tn.tensor, "sx8_pca_b0", tn.bid, tn.xid),
+                    { 32, n_cb }, 0);
+            w.b1 = create_tensor(hparams, buft_list_cpu, buft_list_input, buft_list_output,
+                    buft_list_layer, LLM_TN_IMPL(tn.arch, tn.tensor, "sx8_pca_b1", tn.bid, tn.xid),
+                    { 32, n_cb }, 0);
+            sx8_pca.emplace(tensor, w);
+            sx8_pca_by_name.emplace(tn.str(), w);
+        }
+    }
+
     return tensor;
 }
 
